  eyebrow="Foundation IV"
  title="Worship & the Throne of the Lamb"
  tagline="Why the honor given to the Lamb passes through Him to the One on the throne"
  description="Why the honor given to the Lamb passes through Him to the One on the throne"
>

  <h2>The Argument in One Line</h2>

  <p>
    The book of Revelation shows the Lamb receiving honor in heaven. The elders fall down before Him and sing a new song <em>Revelation 5:8–9</em>. Every creature gives blessing “unto him that sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb” <em>Revelation 5:13</em>. The Lamb stands “in the midst of the throne” <em>Revelation 5:6</em>. In the new city there is “the throne of God and of the Lamb” <em>Revelation 22:1</em>, and the two are its temple <em>Revelation 21:22</em>. Hebrews says the angels worship the Son <em>Hebrews 1:6</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The argument built on these scenes is always the same. Only God may be worshipped. Only God sits on God’s throne. The Lamb is worshipped and shares the throne. Therefore, it is said, the Lamb is God. This piece answers every one of those scenes at once, because they all rest on that single step.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Word “Worship” Means</h2>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">προσκυνέω</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">proskuneō</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">to bow down, to fall before someone in homage — given to God, and also to kings, masters and men of rank</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    The Greek word behind “worship” in these passages is <em>proskuneō</em>, and its Hebrew partner is <em>shachah</em>. Both mean to bow down. Scripture uses them for bowing to Yahuah, and it also uses them for bowing to men.
  </p>

  <p>
    Isaac blessed Jacob: “let nations bow down to thee” <em>Genesis 27:29</em>. The whole assembly “worshipped the LORD [Yahuah], and the king” <em>1 Chronicles 29:20</em> — one word, one act, given to Yahuah and to David together. Abraham “bowed himself” to the Hittites <em>Genesis 23:7</em>, and Joseph’s brothers “bowed down themselves before him” <em>Genesis 42:6</em>. Nebuchadnezzar “worshipped Daniel” <em>Daniel 2:46</em>. In the Master’s parable, the servant who owed a debt “fell down, and worshipped” his king <em>Matthew 18:26</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the word by itself does not prove that the one receiving it is God. Scripture always shows why the honor is given and where it finally goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Bowing Is Not Serving</h2>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">λατρεύω</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">latreuō</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">to serve — the life of sacred service, kept in Scripture for God alone</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    Scripture has a second word, and it never lends that one out. Hebrew has <em>abad</em>, to serve; Greek has <em>latreuō</em>. Bowing is an act done in a moment. Serving is a life given over. The commandment names both: “Thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor serve them” <em>Exodus 20:5</em>. Nebuchadnezzar’s image demanded both as well, and the three Hebrews refused both: “we will not serve thy gods, nor worship the golden image” <em>Daniel 3:18</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Master drew the same line when He answered the enemy: “Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve” <em>Matthew 4:10</em>. The word “only” sits on “serve.” Bowing may go to a king; serving goes to Yahuah alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>Latreuō</em> appears 21 times in the Apostolic writings, and not once is the Son the one served. Paul says “we… worship God in the spirit, and rejoice in Messiah Yahushua” <em>Philippians 3:3</em> — the service goes to God, the rejoicing is in the Messiah. The blood of Messiah cleanses the conscience “to serve the living God” <em>Hebrews 9:14</em>. The Son is the means; the Father is the One served. Even in the throne room, the redeemed “serve him day and night in his temple” — “he that sitteth on the throne” <em>Revelation 7:15</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Believer Is Bowed To as Well</h2>

  <p>
    The plainest test is to ask whether Scripture says the same thing about believers. It does. To the assembly in Philadelphia the Master promised, “I will make them to come and worship before thy feet, and to know that I have loved thee” <em>Revelation 3:9</em>. That is <em>proskuneō</em>, before the feet of faithful believers. If being bowed to made the Lamb God, it would make the overcomers of Philadelphia God too.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the Lamb Is Worthy</h2>

  <p>
    Revelation gives two songs and two reasons. In chapter four the One on the throne is worthy “for thou hast created all things” <em>Revelation 4:11</em>. In chapter five the Lamb is worthy for a different reason: “for thou wast slain, and hast redeemed us to God by thy blood” <em>Revelation 5:9</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Look at those words. The Lamb is honored because He was slain, and He redeemed people “to God” — to Someone other than Himself. The angels sing, “Worthy is the Lamb that was slain to receive power, and riches, and wisdom” <em>Revelation 5:12</em>. He receives. The Father is never praised for having been slain, and He never receives power from anyone. The honor given to the Lamb is the honor of the faithful one who died and was raised, not the honor of the Creator.
  </p>

  <h2>Who Hands the Book to Whom</h2>

  <p>
    Before any song begins, John sees the order of the throne room. One sits on the throne holding a sealed book. Then the Lamb “came and took the book out of the right hand of him that sat upon the throne” <em>Revelation 5:7</em>. The One on the throne gives; the Lamb receives. The whole scene of worship that follows grows out of that act of giving.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter ends where it began. After every creature blesses the One on the throne and the Lamb, the elders “fell down and worshipped him that liveth for ever and ever” <em>Revelation 5:14</em>. The last bow in the scene goes to the One on the throne.
  </p>

  <h2>Honor That Passes Through</h2>

  <p>
    The Master explained this kind of honor Himself. The Father has given all judgment to the Son “that all men should honour the Son, even as they honour the Father. He that honoureth not the Son honoureth not the Father which hath sent him” <em>John 5:22–23</em>. The Son is honored because He was sent. Refusing Him is refusing the One who sent Him.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paul says where that honor ends. Every knee bows at the name of Yahushua, and every tongue confesses Him Master, “to the glory of God the Father” <em>Philippians 2:10–11</em>. Hebrews says the same of the angels: it is God who brings the Son into the world and says, “let all the angels of God worship him” <em>Hebrews 1:6</em>. The Son was “made so much better than the angels” and obtained His name “by inheritance” <em>Hebrews 1:4</em>, and God, “even thy God,” anointed Him above His fellows <em>Hebrews 1:9</em>. The honor is ordered by God, given to the Son, and returns to God.
  </p>

  <p>
    Twice John fell before the angel who showed him the visions, and twice the angel stopped him: “See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant… worship God” <em>Revelation 19:10</em>; <em>Revelation 22:9</em>. The angel did not say bowing belongs to God alone — Lot bowed to angels <em>Genesis 19:1</em>, and Israel bowed to its kings. He said he was John’s fellow servant, a peer with no appointed rank over him. The Lamb is the opposite: the One the Father set on His own throne and gave all authority. The angel turned the bow away because he held no such office; the Lamb receives it because He does — and it still ends in the glory of God.
  </p>

  <h2>Whose Throne Is It?</h2>

  <p>
    The throne in Revelation belongs to the Father. The Master says so: “To him that overcometh will I grant to sit with me in my throne, even as I also overcame, and am set down with my Father in his throne” <em>Revelation 3:21</em>. The throne is “his” — the Father’s. The Son sits there because He overcame and was set down. And He promises the same seat to believers who overcome.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not new. Of Solomon, Scripture says, “Solomon sat on the throne of the LORD [Yahuah] as king instead of David his father” <em>1 Chronicles 29:23</em>. A man sat on Yahuah’s throne as Yahuah’s appointed king. Sitting on the throne by appointment makes a king, not a second God.
  </p>

  <p>
    And the appointment has an end in view. When all things are put under the Son, “then shall the Son also himself be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all” <em>1 Corinthians 15:28</em>. A throne held until it is handed back is a throne held by gift.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the Lamb stands “in the midst of the throne” <em>Revelation 5:6</em>, He stands there with the living creatures and the elders around Him, and He is the one who shepherds the people: the Lamb in the midst of the throne “shall feed them, and shall lead them unto living fountains of waters” <em>Revelation 7:17</em>. He is the King’s shepherd at the center of the court.
  </p>

  <h2>One Name, One “He”</h2>

  <p>
    Some point out that after naming God and the Lamb together, Revelation uses a singular word. “The kingdoms of this world are become the kingdoms of our Lord, and of his Messiah; and he shall reign for ever and ever” <em>Revelation 11:15</em>. “The throne of God and of the Lamb shall be in it; and his servants shall serve him: and they shall see his face; and his name shall be in their foreheads” <em>Revelation 22:3–4</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    But read who “he” is. In Revelation 11:15 the Messiah is “his Messiah” — the anointed one who belongs to the Lord, just as the Psalm says, “against the LORD [Yahuah], and against his anointed” <em>Psalm 2:2</em>. In Revelation 22:4 the name in their foreheads is named elsewhere in the same book: they have “his Father’s name written in their foreheads” <em>Revelation 14:1</em>. The word for “serve” there is <em>latreuō</em>, the service kept for God alone. The singular “he” is the Father, whose throne it is and whose name His servants carry. The Lamb reigns with Him as His appointed King.
  </p>

  <h2>The Temple and the Lamp</h2>

  <p>
    John says of the new city, “the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are the temple of it” <em>Revelation 21:22</em>. The very next verse explains the roles: “the glory of God did lighten it, and the Lamb is the light thereof” <em>Revelation 21:23</em>. The word for light there is <em>luchnos</em>, a lamp. God’s glory is the light; the Lamb is the lamp that carries it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Believers are called a temple too: “ye are the temple of God” <em>1 Corinthians 3:16</em>, and the overcomer becomes “a pillar in the temple of my God” <em>Revelation 3:12</em>. Being named as part of God’s dwelling does not make a man God, and it does not make the Lamb God. The same goes for the believers who will be “priests of God and of Messiah” <em>Revelation 20:6</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Test for Every Throne and Worship Scene</h2>

  <p>
    Every scene of worship and throne in Revelation can be read with three plain questions. First, why is the Lamb honored — for creating, or for being slain and redeeming people to God? Second, who gives and who receives — the book, the throne, the power? Third, where does the honor finally go? The answers are always the same: the Lamb is honored for His sacrifice, He receives from the One on the throne, and the last bow goes to the Father.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Lamb is worthy, and Scripture commands that He be honored. But He is honored as the slain and risen Son whom the Father seated on His throne. The worship passes through Him to the One who sent Him.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Bible ends with that order fixed forever. From “the throne of God and of the Lamb” flows a pure river of water of life <em>Revelation 22:1</em>. The throne is God’s, the Lamb is given His place in it, and the servants who stand before it carry one name on their foreheads: the name of the Father <em>Revelation 14:1</em>.
  </p>


